# The app host: boot the routed app, env-abstracted. The SAME `route(host, path)` dispatcher drives every side; only
# this `host` differs per environment (the native-env mechanism picks it). The browser mounts the matched page on the
# document body and re-renders on navigation; the server starts an HTTP server that renders each request through
# `route` and responds with HTML (SSR); macOS, iOS and Android mount it on a window (./toolkit/host); a Rust binary
# runs it in the terminal (./rust/host). This is the one client/server seam -- the app author writes neither.
#
# THIS FILE is the answer for a target with no screen of its own, a plain Swift or Kotlin program: it mounts nothing,
# as the page contract's fallback keeps no history (../../dom/native/page.tree). It was a signature with no body, which
# every routed program reached through its `boot` and which a call may no longer reach (a stub, not a host).

load ../../dom/view
  find view

task host
  take route
    like task
      take host, like view
      take path, like text
  take port, like number
  save nothing, code 0
